# Bitcoin Lifeboat — Recovery Readiness Report

> Bitcoin Lifeboat is not a wallet, not a custody service, not a
> seed phrase manager, not an inheritance legal service, and not a
> recovery company. It is a free, open-source diagnostic tool that
> helps you test whether your recovery plan works.

## 1. Status

**Status: Not Ready** (score 25/100)

No critical failures, 10 warnings.

- Wallet type: multisig — wsh(sortedmulti), 2-of-3
- Network: testnet
- Survives loss of 1 signer: yes
- Survives loss of 2 signers: no — the remaining signers would fall below the threshold
- Survives loss of the descriptor backup: yes, if the xpubs are retained

## 2. What passed

- Descriptor parsed successfully
- Descriptor checksum is valid
- Multisig threshold identified as 2-of-3

## 3. What needs attention

- **No recovery drill in the last 12 months** — A recovery plan that has never been rehearsed end-to-end is untested. Hardware, software, and memory all change over time. _(W-NO-RECENT-DRILL)_
- **Hardware wallet not exercised recently** — A hardware signer that has not signed anything in over a year may have a dead battery, corrupted firmware, or a forgotten PIN. _(W-NO-HW-TEST)_

## 4. What failed

_None._

## 5. What is missing

- **Change descriptor missing** — A complete recovery backup should include both receive and change descriptors. Without the change descriptor, an empty wallet restore will not see funds returned to change addresses. _(W-NO-CHANGE-DESC)_
- **No wallet birth height documented** — Without a creation date or block height, a recovery wallet must scan the whole chain, which is slow and can miss funds if the gap limit is exceeded. _(W-NO-BIRTH-HEIGHT)_
- **Gap limit not documented** — If you have used many addresses, a default gap limit (20) may stop a recovery wallet before it finds all your funds. _(W-NO-GAP-LIMIT)_
- **No known address provided** — You did not provide a known address to compare against the derived range, so Lifeboat could not confirm the descriptor produces addresses you recognize. _(W-NO-KNOWN-ADDRESS)_
- **No printed descriptor backup** — A descriptor stored only on a computer can be lost with the device. A printed or stamped copy survives disk failure and software changes. _(W-NO-PRINTED-BACKUP)_
- **No heir instructions written** — Without written instructions, the people you intend to inherit your bitcoin may not know the materials exist or how to use them. _(W-NO-HEIR-INSTRUCTIONS)_
- **No emergency contact named** — No trusted person is recorded to help an heir who gets stuck during recovery. _(W-NO-EMERGENCY-CONTACT)_
- **Wallet software not documented** — If you do not record which wallet software your setup depends on, an heir may not know how to load the descriptor. _(W-WALLET-SW-UNDOCUMENTED)_

## 6. What to do next

1. Export your change descriptor. _(est. 5 min)_
2. Write down your wallet's creation date or block height. _(est. 5 min)_
3. Document your wallet's gap limit. _(est. 5 min)_
4. Provide one known address from this wallet to compare. _(est. 2 min)_
5. Print two copies of the descriptor backup. _(est. 10 min)_
6. Run a Disaster Drill in Lifeboat v0.3 when available. _(est. future)_
7. Write recovery instructions for your heirs. _(est. 30 min)_
8. Name a trusted emergency contact. _(est. 5 min)_
9. Sign a test transaction with each hardware device. _(est. 20 min)_
10. Document which wallet software your setup needs. _(est. 5 min)_

## 7. What NOT to do

- Do not email the descriptor.
- Do not store the descriptor in cloud notes apps.
- Do not photograph this report and send it on chat.

## 8. When to run this again

Run this readiness check again by **2026-11-28** — within 12 months, or sooner if your score is below 70.

